1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,233
Good day. Imagine spending 20 coins
to make a gift, then selling it for 24.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:05,500
You gain 4 coins.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,400
If you keep repeating this, was that reward planned?

4
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:12,900
LoopGuard helps game designers answer that
question before changing their prices or rewards.

5
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:15,100
Here is how that example works.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,233
20 coins buys 2 ribbons.

7
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:21,467
The player turns both ribbons into
one gift and sells it for 24 coins.

8
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:25,867
We call that a complete sequence and a route
because it starts and ends with coins.

9
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:31,933
The problem is whether its 4 coin gain can
repeat in a way that designer never intended.

10
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,267
LoopGuard gives the designer 3 kinds of advice.

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,833
Correct means adjust a problematic price or reward.

12
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:41,400
Preserve means keep a planned reward with known limits.

13
00:00:41,633 --> 00:00:45,000
Investigate means check missing
information or a possible weakness.

14
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:49,400
Each recommendation includes a reason
and the designer still decides what to change.

15
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,533
Our setting is an invented Sanrio fair.

16
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:57,600
My Melody Shop makes the gift, Kuromi and
Keroppi handle a separate charm exchange.

17
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:00,200
Cinnamoroll and Pompompurin represent a café.

18
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:04,000
These characters help us remember the shops we invented,

19
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:06,700
their prices and rules for this classroom project.

20
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,133
That designer uses Streamlit,
our Python application's interface,

21
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:12,267
to enter prices and read recommendations.

22
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:16,400
Python calculates the exchanges and checks 35 written rules.

23
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:21,200
KNIME is a visual workflow tool that makes
separate decisions from Python's calculated facts.

24
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:24,600
Maersk will now explain how those facts become advice.

25
00:01:24,833 --> 00:01:25,867
Start with the gift.

26
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:28,367
What does it cost and what does it return?

27
00:01:28,733 --> 00:01:31,867
The designer also enters whether the reward is planned,

28
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,567
how often a trade is allowed, and how long players must wait.

29
00:01:35,967 --> 00:01:38,467
A blank limit means we do not know.

30
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:42,300
Entering zero explicitly means that there is no limit.

31
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,533
Python checks these entries,

32
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:51,000
then connects the trades to find
routes like our ribbon-to-gift example.

33
00:01:51,533 --> 00:01:54,900
It calculates what each complete route costs and returns.

34
00:01:55,533 --> 00:02:00,833
Next, the rules ask whether the gain is
intended and what restricts repetition.

35
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:03,233
That separates two questions.

36
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:05,100
Can the player earn coins?

37
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:07,533
And should the designer allow that reward?

38
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,633
Python must respect complete trades.

39
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,533
If a gift requires two ribbons,

40
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,733
half a price cannot count as a finished gift.

41
00:02:17,133 --> 00:02:22,500
The code finds the smallest combination of full
trades that works and keeps fraction exact,

42
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,267
so rounding does not create a false profit.

43
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:29,500
The rules turn the result into advice.

44
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:35,300
If a profitable route is an unintended
and has no limits, flag it for correction.

45
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:38,033
If it's planned and limited, preserve it.

46
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,667
If important facts are missing, ask for evidence.

47
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:44,067
This is our expert system.

48
00:02:44,167 --> 00:02:48,067
Written decision rules apply the facts
with the reasoning recorded.

49
00:02:50,033 --> 00:02:55,800
Our decision code also distinguishes an unlimited
planned reward from unlimited accidental one.

50
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,833
It rates the planned reward as high
concern and accidental reward as a critical.

51
00:03:01,567 --> 00:03:04,167
A planned limited reward can be preserved.

52
00:03:04,533 --> 00:03:06,967
Missing information checks happen first,

53
00:03:07,333 --> 00:03:11,033
so an unknown limit is never silently treated as unlimited.

54
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,833
Rey will now apply this to our example.

55
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:21,067
Our gift turns 20 coins into two
ribbons, then one gift, then 24 coins.

56
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:27,500
The designer marks the four coin gain as unintended
and confirms there are no limits or waiting times.

57
00:03:27,867 --> 00:03:30,100
LoopGuard flags it as critical.

58
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:35,333
Both the extra coins and the unrestricted
repetition explain why it needs correction.

59
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:36,967
Now consider charms.

60
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:41,967
Eight coins buy a stamp, which
becomes a charm worth 12 coins.

61
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:47,467
Each account can buy only two stamps daily,
but can pass items to other accounts.

62
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:50,533
Could several accounts combine purchases?

63
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:54,400
LoopGuard flags that concern for investigation.

64
00:03:54,633 --> 00:03:58,167
It has not proven that players can bypass the limit.

65
00:03:59,367 --> 00:04:04,267
The Café shows why profit alone is not a problem.

66
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:09,367
Ten coins buy two beans, which make a latte worth 14 coins.

67
00:04:09,767 --> 00:04:11,300
This reward is planned.

68
00:04:11,567 --> 00:04:16,267
The Café allows only three payouts daily across the whole fair.

69
00:04:16,567 --> 00:04:19,333
Each player waits an hour between payouts.

70
00:04:19,533 --> 00:04:22,700
LoopGuard recommends keeping this controlled reward.

71
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,733
Now suppose the designer leaves the gift's limits blank.

72
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,600
We still know it earns four coins, but
we cannot tell how often it can repeat.

73
00:04:32,867 --> 00:04:35,500
LoopGuard requests more information.

74
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:38,833
Its daily simulation says no estimate,

75
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:43,133
which means insufficient information, not zero earnings.

76
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:47,200
Alvin will show how a designer can use these decisions.

77
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,600
Thanks Rey.

78
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:52,733
In Streamlit, the designer enters or loads the shop prices,

79
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:56,700
runs a check, and reads why a route was flagged.

80
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:00,933
They can edit the prices, preview a fix, or undo a change.

81
00:05:01,133 --> 00:05:04,500
They can also export the project to a file

82
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:09,167
so their entered prices and rules can be reopened later.

83
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:14,400
For the gift, we can preview reducing
the selling price to 20 coins.

84
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:18,200
Spending 20, and receiving 20 removes the gain.

85
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:22,233
The preview checks a copy before applying changes.

86
00:05:22,533 --> 00:05:24,533
A daily limit works differently.

87
00:05:25,033 --> 00:05:29,433
The 4 coin gain remains, but it can happen fewer times.

88
00:05:29,567 --> 00:05:33,600
We also estimate a day's earning before that correction.

89
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:39,867
Of 10 requested runs, gifts complete
all 10, earning 40 extra coins.

90
00:05:40,033 --> 00:05:43,600
Charms stop at 2 runs, earning 8.

91
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:47,967
The café stops at 3, earning 12.

92
00:05:47,967 --> 00:05:50,433
We assume one account enough starting

93
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:54,467
money and supplies and initially unused shared limits.

94
00:05:54,633 --> 00:05:57,500
Earning are not reused to fund more trades.

95
00:06:00,067 --> 00:06:05,367
KNIME makes a separate decision
from the same calculated facts.

96
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,467
Python exports facts such as profits and limits.

97
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:14,733
KNIME reads them, applies rules
to choose a category and urgency,

98
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,533
then saves its decisions.

99
00:06:16,533 --> 00:06:20,767
Streamlit checks that both sets of results

100
00:06:20,767 --> 00:06:25,600
cover the same routes and current
prices and rules before comparing them.

101
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:28,333
We ran KNIME manually.

102
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:32,567
All four workflows, steps executed, and its six decisions

103
00:06:32,567 --> 00:06:37,333
match the expected results for our
starting Friendship Fair example.

104
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:40,133
That verifies this particular run.

105
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:45,967
Python still handles finding routes, calculating
trades, preview fixes, and simulation.

106
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:48,900
Nads will explain our wider checks.

107
00:06:49,167 --> 00:06:50,100
Thanks, Alvin.

108
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:52,433
In here, we prepared four versions of the fair.

109
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,067
First, Friendship Fair is the starting example.

110
00:06:55,367 --> 00:06:58,267
The Balanced Market corrects the unintended gains.

111
00:06:58,567 --> 00:07:03,333
Missing Telemetry deliberately removes information
so that the system should ask questions.

112
00:07:03,667 --> 00:07:07,200
Festival Rush raises the gift payout and removes café limits.

113
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:10,067
Changing the facts lets us check

114
00:07:10,067 --> 00:07:13,833
whether the same rules produce
the appropriate different advice.

115
00:07:13,967 --> 00:07:18,433
October 25th record reports 116 passing tests.

116
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:23,200
These are calculations, decisions, data
exchange, and Streamlit interactions.

117
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,533
These functions behave as expected on those tests.

118
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:29,867
To learn whether game designers
actually find the system useful,

119
00:07:29,967 --> 00:07:33,133
we still need trials with people and realistic game data.

120
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:41,867
This prototype checks the routes of up
to six trades within fixed search limits.

121
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:45,667
Each trade converts one-of-a-kind item into one other kind.

122
00:07:45,967 --> 00:07:50,667
Changing demand, scarce stock,
and random rewards are outside its scope.

123
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:54,233
A clean result applies only to the entered
moves and search performed.

124
00:07:54,233 --> 00:07:58,300
Further KNIME checks and game
designer feedback are the next steps.

125
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:02,167
Here we followed the gift from a four coin gain to a correction.

126
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:05,900
Reducing its selling price to 20 coins removes that gain.

127
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:11,100
Keeping the original price but leaving limits unknown
produces a request for information instead.

128
00:08:11,767 --> 00:08:16,133
LoopGuard helps designers understand
the recommendation before changing their game's rewards.

129
00:08:16,533 --> 00:08:17,133
Thank you.

130
00:08:19,367 --> 00:08:20,000
Then here...
